All five variants (mixbench-cuda, mixbench-hip, mixbench-opencl, mixbench-sycl, mixbench-cpu) have been characterized and recorded as separate traces. Summary:

- **mixbench-cuda (cuda)**: single-process, single-GPU CUDA microbenchmark, no MPI/network communication, streaming memory-access kernel with configurable compute intensity (roofline sweep), no multi-GPU, no checkpoint/IO.
- **mixbench-hip (rocm)**: same design ported to HIP/ROCm, single device (index 0 fixed), single process.
- **mixbench-opencl (opencl)**: same design, single OpenCL device selected via CLI arg, single process.
- **mixbench-sycl (sycl)**: same design, single SYCL device selected via CLI arg (can target CPU/GPU/FPGA backends but only one per run), single process.
- **mixbench-cpu (cpu-openmp)**: OpenMP-threaded CPU version, runs benchmark first at full thread count then at half thread count, no MPI, no accelerator, streaming access pattern over a large buffer per thread.

All variants share: no MPI, no network communication (model=none), no shared-memory windows, no parallel I/O, no checkpointing, single-node topology, no ordered dependency between execution units (embarrassingly parallel streaming kernels). The defining characteristic across all variants is that the benchmark deliberately sweeps operational/arithmetic intensity from memory-bound to compute-bound configurations to build a roofline model, so `memory.bound_by`/`arithmetic_intensity` are not fixed properties of the code but swept parameters — this is noted in each trace rather than forced into a single enum value.
